NeoBoard's chart system is plugin-based. Each chart type (bar, line, pie, etc.) is a self-contained plugin that registers itself with the chart registry. You can add new chart types by creating a single plugin file.

## Quick Start

Create a file at `app/src/plugins/your-chart.tsx`:

```tsx
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";
import { Skeleton } from "@neoboard/components";
import { defineChartPlugin } from "./registry";
import { useEChartsClick, type PluginProps } from "./utils";

// Lazy-load your chart component (ECharts needs client-side only)
const MyChart = dynamic(
  () => import("@neoboard/components").then((m) => ({ default: m.MyChart })),
  { ssr: false, loading: () => <Skeleton className="w-full h-full" /> },
);

function MyChartPlugin({ data, settings, onChartClick }: PluginProps) {
  const onClick = useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data);
  return (
    <MyChart
      data={data ?? []}
      showLegend={settings.showLegend as boolean}
      onClick={onClick}
    />
  );
}

export const myChartPlugin = defineChartPlugin({
  type: "my-chart",
  label: "My Chart",
  component: MyChartPlugin,
  transform: (rows) => rows, // transform raw query rows to chart data
  compatibleWith: ["neo4j", "postgresql"],
  capabilities: {
    supportsClickAction: true,
    supportsStyling: false,
    isECharts: true,
    requiresQuery: true,
  },
  queryHint: "Return label and value columns.\nExample: RETURN name, count",
  options: [
    {
      key: "showLegend",
      label: "Show Legend",
      type: "boolean",
      default: true,
      category: "Labels",
      description: "Show the chart legend.",
    },
  ],
});
```

Then register it in `app/src/plugins/index.ts`:

```ts
import { myChartPlugin } from "./my-chart";

// Add to the BUILT_IN_PLUGINS array:
const BUILT_IN_PLUGINS = [
  // ... existing plugins
  myChartPlugin,
];
```

That's it. Your chart appears in the widget editor's chart type picker.

## Plugin Config Reference

```ts
defineChartPlugin({
  // Required
  type: string,           // unique ID: "heatmap", "funnel", etc.
  label: string,          // display name in the UI
  component: React.FC,    // React component that renders the chart
  transform: (data) => T, // convert raw query rows to chart's data shape

  // Optional
  transformWithMapping?: (data, mapping) => T,  // with column mapping
  validate?: (data) => string | null,           // return error or null
  options?: ChartOptionDef[],                   // chart options panel entries
  queryHint?: string,                           // shown in query editor
  compatibleWith?: ConnectorType[],             // ["neo4j", "postgresql"]
  stylingTargets?: { value, label }[],          // enables rule-based styling
  enrichClickEvent?: (event, row) => event,     // customize click payload

  // Capabilities (all have sensible defaults)
  capabilities?: {
    supportsClickAction: boolean,  // default: true
    supportsStyling: boolean,      // default: true if stylingTargets set
    isECharts: boolean,            // default: false
    requiresQuery: boolean,        // default: true
  },
});
```

## Plugin Component Props

Every plugin component receives these props via `PluginProps` (import from `./utils`):

| Prop | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `data` | `unknown` | Transformed query result data |
| `settings` | `Record<string, unknown>` | User-configured chart options |
| `onChartClick` | `(row) => void` | Raw click callback (use `useEChartsClick()` for ECharts) |
| `stylingRules` | `StylingRule[]` | Conditional color rules |
| `paramValues` | `Record<string, unknown>` | Current parameter values |
| `colorScales` | `ColorScaleConfig[]` | Gradient color scales |
| `connectionId` | `string` | Current connector ID |
| `widgetId` | `string` | Widget identifier |
| `resultId` | `string` | Query result cache ID |
| `query` | `string` | Current query text |
| `autoFit` | `boolean` | Auto-fit on resize |
| `colorThresholds` | `string` | Legacy threshold JSON |

## Chart Options

Options define what users can configure in the Chart Options panel:

```ts
interface ChartOptionDef {
  key: string;          // settings key (e.g. "showLegend")
  label: string;        // UI label
  type: "boolean" | "select" | "text" | "number" | "column-multi-select";
  default: unknown;     // default value
  category: string;     // group heading (e.g. "Layout", "Labels", "Style")
  options?: { label: string; value: string }[];  // for "select" type
  description?: string; // tooltip text
}
```

## ECharts vs Custom Charts

**ECharts-based charts** (bar, line, pie, gauge, etc.):
- Use `dynamic(() => import("@neoboard/components")...)` with `ssr: false`
- Use `useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data)` to wrap the click handler
- Set `capabilities.isECharts: true`

**Non-ECharts charts** (graph, map, table, form):
- Import components directly (no dynamic import needed if not using browser APIs)
- Use `onChartClick` directly (no wrapping needed)
- Set `capabilities.isECharts: false`

**Content-only widgets** (markdown, iframe):
- Set `capabilities.requiresQuery: false`
- `transform` returns `null`
- No click action or styling

## Example: Adding a Heatmap

```tsx
// app/src/plugins/heatmap.tsx
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";
import { Skeleton } from "@neoboard/components";
import { defineChartPlugin } from "./registry";
import { useEChartsClick, type PluginProps } from "./utils";

const HeatmapChart = dynamic(
  () => import("../components/charts/heatmap-chart").then((m) => ({ default: m.HeatmapChart })),
  { ssr: false, loading: () => <Skeleton className="w-full h-full" /> },
);

function HeatmapPlugin({ data, settings, onChartClick }: PluginProps) {
  const onClick = useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data);
  return (
    <HeatmapChart
      data={data ?? []}
      xField={settings.xField as string}
      yField={settings.yField as string}
      valueField={settings.valueField as string}
      onClick={onClick}
    />
  );
}

export const heatmapPlugin = defineChartPlugin({
  type: "heatmap",
  label: "Heatmap",
  component: HeatmapPlugin,
  transform: (rows) => rows,
  compatibleWith: ["neo4j", "postgresql"],
  capabilities: { supportsClickAction: true, isECharts: true, requiresQuery: true, supportsStyling: false },
  queryHint: "Return 3 columns: x, y, value.\nExample: RETURN day, hour, count",
  options: [
    { key: "xField", label: "X Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
    { key: "yField", label: "Y Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
    { key: "valueField", label: "Value Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
  ],
});
```

This guide walks through adding a new chart type to NeoBoard, from registration to rendering.

## Overview

Adding a chart type involves:
1. Define the chart type identifier
2. Create the chart component in `component/`
3. Register a transform function in the chart registry
4. Add a data validator
5. Add to the chart type picker UI
6. Write tests

## Step 1: Define the Chart Type

Add your chart type to the `ChartType` union in `component/src/charts/types.ts`:

```typescript
export type ChartType =
  | "bar"
  | "line"
  | "pie"
  | "single-value"
  | "table"
  | "graph"
  | "map"
  | "json"
  | "your-new-chart"; // Add here
```

## Step 2: Create the Chart Component

Create a new file in `component/src/charts/`:

```typescript
// component/src/charts/your-new-chart.tsx
import type { BaseChartProps } from "./types";

interface YourNewChartProps extends BaseChartProps {
  // Add chart-specific props
}

export function YourNewChart({ options, loading, error, className }: YourNewChartProps) {
  // Use ECharts BaseChart or build a custom renderer
  // ...
}
```

**Important**: Use modular ECharts imports only:
```typescript
import * as echarts from "echarts/core";
import { BarChart } from "echarts/charts";
// Never: import * as echarts from "echarts"
```

## Step 3: Register in Chart Registry

Add your chart to `app/src/lib/chart-registry.ts`:

```typescript
registry.set("your-new-chart", {
  label: "Your New Chart",
  icon: ChartIcon, // from lucide-react
  transform: (data, settings) => {
    // Transform query results into ECharts options
    return { /* EChartsOption */ };
  },
  validate: (data) => {
    // Return true if data shape is compatible
    return data.length > 0 && data[0].hasOwnProperty("value");
  },
  defaultSettings: {
    // Default chart options
  },
});
```

## Step 4: Add to Chart Type Picker

Update the widget editor to include your chart in the type selection dropdown.

## Step 5: Dynamic Import

Charts must be lazy-loaded with SSR disabled:

```typescript
// In the page renderer
const YourNewChart = dynamic(
  () => import("@neoboard/components").then((m) => m.YourNewChart),
  { ssr: false }
);
```

## Step 6: Write Tests

1. **Chart registry test** — verify transform and validate functions
2. **Component test** — render the chart with mock data (in `component/`)
3. **E2E test** — create a widget with the new chart type

NeoBoard's connector system is plugin-based. Each database type (Neo4j, PostgreSQL) is a self-contained plugin. You can add support for new databases by creating a connector plugin.

## Quick Start

Create a plugin file in the connection package:

```ts
// connection/src/my-database/plugin.ts
import type { ConnectorPlugin } from "../generalized/connector-plugin";
import type { AuthConfig } from "../generalized/interfaces";
import { MyDatabaseConnectionModule } from "./MyDatabaseConnectionModule";

export const myDatabasePlugin: ConnectorPlugin = {
  type: "my-database",
  label: "My Database",
  category: "database",
  queryLanguage: "sql",
  supportsWrite: true,
  supportsGraphData: false,
  allowedProtocols: ["mydb:", "mydb+ssl:"],
  uriPlaceholder: "mydb://localhost:5555/mydb",
  databasePlaceholder: "mydb",

  createModule(authConfig: AuthConfig, advancedOptions?: Record<string, unknown>) {
    return new MyDatabaseConnectionModule(authConfig, advancedOptions);
  },
};
```

Then register it in `connection/src/connector-registry.ts`:

```ts
import { myDatabasePlugin } from "./my-database/plugin";

// Add after the existing registrations:
registry.register(myDatabasePlugin);
```

## Connector Plugin Reference

```ts
interface ConnectorPlugin {
  // Required
  type: string;           // unique ID: "mysql", "mongodb", "rest-api"
  label: string;          // display name: "MySQL", "MongoDB"
  category: "database" | "graph" | "api" | "file";
  createModule(auth, opts): ConnectionModule;  // factory for the driver

  // Optional
  supportsGraphData?: boolean;    // can produce nodes/edges? (default: false)
  supportsWrite?: boolean;        // supports INSERT/CREATE? (default: false)
  queryLanguage?: string;         // "cypher", "sql", or custom
  allowedProtocols?: string[];    // URI validation: ["mysql:", "mysql+ssl:"]
  uriPlaceholder?: string;        // shown in connection form
  databasePlaceholder?: string;   // shown in database field
}
```

## Implementing a ConnectionModule

Your connector must extend the abstract `ConnectionModule` class:

```ts
import { ConnectionModule } from "../generalized/ConnectionModule";
import { AuthenticationModule } from "../generalized/AuthenticationModule";
import type { ConnectionConfig, QueryCallback, QueryParams } from "../generalized/interfaces";

export class MyDatabaseConnectionModule extends ConnectionModule {
  authModule: AuthenticationModule;

  constructor(authConfig: AuthConfig, advancedOptions?: Record<string, unknown>) {
    super();
    // Initialize your driver here
    this.authModule = new MyDatabaseAuthModule(authConfig);
  }

  async runQuery<T>(
    queryParams: QueryParams,
    callbacks: QueryCallback<T>,
    config: ConnectionConfig,
  ): Promise<void> {
    // Handle empty query
    if (this.handleEmptyQuery(queryParams.query, callbacks)) return;

    try {
      // Execute the query using your driver
      const results = await this.executeQuery(queryParams.query, queryParams.params);

      // Convert results to NeoBoard's record format
      const records = results.map(row => ({
        // Map each column to key-value pairs
        ...row,
      }));

      // Call the success callback
      if (callbacks.setRecords) {
        callbacks.setRecords(records as T);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      if (callbacks.setError) {
        callbacks.setError(error);
      }
    }
  }

  async checkConnection(config?: ConnectionConfig): Promise<boolean> {
    try {
      // Try to connect and run a simple query
      await this.executeQuery("SELECT 1");
      return true;
    } catch {
      return false;
    }
  }
}
```

## Built-in Connectors

NeoBoard ships with two built-in connector plugins:

### Neo4j
- **Type:** `"neo4j"`
- **Category:** `"graph"`
- **Language:** Cypher
- **Graph data:** Yes (nodes, relationships, paths)
- **Write:** Yes (CREATE, MERGE, DELETE)
- **Protocols:** `bolt:`, `neo4j:`, `bolt+s:`, `neo4j+s:`

### PostgreSQL
- **Type:** `"postgresql"`
- **Category:** `"database"`
- **Language:** SQL
- **Graph data:** No
- **Write:** Yes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE)
- **Protocols:** `postgresql:`, `postgres:`

## Registry API

```ts
import {
  registerConnector,
  getConnector,
  getAllConnectors,
  createConnectionModule,
} from "@neoboard/connection/src/connector-registry";

// Register a new connector
registerConnector(myPlugin);

// Look up a connector
const plugin = getConnector("mysql");

// List all registered connectors
const all = getAllConnectors(); // [neo4jPlugin, postgresPlugin, ...]

// Create a connection module (replaces the old factory)
const module = createConnectionModule("neo4j", authConfig, advancedOptions);
```

